--- body ---
## Context

Author asked directly: "Are we testing against the ticket 029 gold adversarial
library?" No -- every adversarial test in this wave (0690, 0695) runs against
synthetic host mocks in a Node `vm` sandbox. None of them exercise the sitter
against real pathological documents: multi-hundred-page books, mixed scripts
(the Menagerie's EN/FR/VI MUST tier plus SHOULD-tier Arabic/Russian/Chinese/
German/Hindi/Spanish), malformed or oversized attachments, or group-library
membership. Mock tests prove the scheduler's control flow; they cannot prove
the census/extraction pipeline survives what the corpus was specifically
built to contain.

Ticket 0029 is the tracker for that corpus. The library exists now: group
6659303 on zotero.org holds 103 documents in collection 3AY48MA5, 114
top-level items and 125 attachments with their bytes on the server, in the
nine languages of R7 and with the deliberate legacy encodings among them
(windows-1252, windows-1251, koi8-r, gbk, big5). The earlier blocker on
child 0632, which stood for the absence of any injectable fixture library,
is gone; this ticket's work can start.

Two things about the corpus that bear on this validation. Its documents were
chosen to be hard: multi-hundred-page scans, an un-OCR'd volume, a DjVu the
extractor never reads, attachments in formats Zotero never extracts, and
deliberately mis-declared metadata. And the group is PublicClosed, so file
bytes need a member's key, which the census path must handle rather than
read as an absent file.

## Work

- Run the sitter's full census -> candidate -> extraction -> accounting
  pipeline against the live fixture, in a real (or realistic-scale) Zotero
  profile that has joined group 6659303.
- Specifically watch for: a census that never terminates or takes
  disproportionately long on the corpus's largest documents; extraction
  hangs or crashes on any SHOULD-tier script; the 0699 accounting fix
  (every non-indexed document lands in exactly one bucket) actually holding
  on documents that fail for real reasons, not synthetic ones; group-library
  attachments handled correctly by the admission/inspection logic.
- Wait for the sitter run already in flight to complete before starting;
  do not interrupt it, and record what it was doing, so its own pass is
  evidence rather than a disturbance.
- After the census+extraction pass, analyse what it left behind rather than
  only what it reported: the debug journal and Zotero's own log for the
  transitions the sitter recorded, and the filesystem for the state it
  created (storage directories touched, caches written, any residue outside
  the declared locations, per R15's inventory clause). Reconcile the three
  accounts -- the sitter's counters, the log, and the filesystem -- and name
  every disagreement.
- This is an integration/acceptance validation, not a unit-test replacement
  -- it does not retire or duplicate 0690/0695's mock-based tests.

## Acceptance

- [ ] Sitter runs a full census+extraction pass over the live Menagerie
      fixture without hanging or crashing.
- [ ] Final failure/success counts reconcile against the corpus's known
      document count (ties to 0699's reconciliation test, on real data).
- [ ] Any script/format that the sitter cannot handle is named explicitly,
      not silently miscounted or dropped.
